$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a5ad26b-eab5-5fd9-938d-c3f9865b2926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503703 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7w}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7w\\cdot 5{z}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{35w{z}^{3}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503731 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503731 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503731 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{x}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{8{x}^{2}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503731 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b49efc81-0ddf-5a4e-8d55-47ad95f0b7d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503731 #edb/ref 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{8{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x\\cdot 8{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot {x}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(2x)^{3}} & = \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503733 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503733 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503733 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503733 #edb/ref 1071 "If $z\\ge 0,$ then $\\sqrt{3z}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{6}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503733 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b57aff47-fce6-506a-bd4c-d85de1a296a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503733 #edb/ref 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3z}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3z\\cdot 5{z}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{z}^{6}\\cdot 15z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({z}^{3})}^{2}\\cdot 15z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({z}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{15z} & = \\\\ |{z}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{15z} & = \\\\ |z{|}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{15z} & =\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ge 0,$ we have $|z|=z,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n|z{|}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{15z}={z}^{3}\\sqrt{15z}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503743 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt{16w}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9{w}^{3}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d17d0de9-c925-52f5-bd49-81ba61ecf89c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503743 #edb/ref 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{16w}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9{w}^{3}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{16w\\cdot 9{w}^{3}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{144{w}^{4}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{(12{w}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ 3|12{w}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |12|\\cdot |{w}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 12\\cdot |{w}^{2}| & = \\\\ 36\\cdot |{w}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot |{w}^{2}| & =36{w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
